data.source:
A brick can hold a form: a contact form, a newsletter sign-up, a quote request. Mortar doesn't add a form API for this. The brick renders a plain HTML form, a handler on the server takes the post, and the browser is sent back to the page with the outcome. That works on [pages without JavaScript](/docs/no-javascript), and a refresh never posts twice.

The example site (`examples/site`) has a working contact form, at `/contact`. Its pieces:

- `src/bricks/contact-form.tsx`: the brick. Editors set its heading, intro, button label and the message shown once it's sent.
- `src/server/contact.ts`: the handler. It checks the post, emails it through a `Mailer` and redirects back.
- `src/middleware.ts`: runs the handler before Mortar's middleware.
- `src/contact.ts`: what both sides share (the path, field names and limits), with no UI or server code in it.

## The brick

The form posts to a fixed path, with the page's own path in a hidden field so the handler knows where to send the visitor back. The brick reads the outcome from the URL the handler redirects to.

```tsx
import { defineBrick, fields } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { useLocation, useSearchParams } from '@solidjs/router';
import { Show } from 'solid-js';

export const contactForm = defineBrick({
  name: 'contact-form',
  label: 'Contact form',
  schema: {
    heading: fields.text({ label: 'Heading', default: 'Get in touch' }),
    buttonLabel: fields.text({ label: 'Button label', required: true, default: 'Send message' }),
    sentMessage: fields.textarea({ label: 'After sending', default: 'Thanks! We will reply by email.' }),
  },
  component: (props) => {
    const location = useLocation();
    const [search] = useSearchParams();
    return (
      <section id="contact">
        <h2>{props.data.heading}</h2>
        <Show when={search.contact !== 'sent'} fallback={<p role="status">{props.data.sentMessage}</p>}>
          <Show when={search.contact === 'invalid'}>
            <p role="alert">Please fill in your name, a valid email address and a message.</p>
          </Show>
          <form method="post" action="/contact">
            <input type="hidden" name="return" value={location.pathname} />
            <label>Name <input name="name" required maxlength="100" /></label>
            <label>Email <input name="email" type="email" required maxlength="254" /></label>
            <label>Message <textarea name="message" required maxlength="5000" /></label>
            <button type="submit">{props.data.buttonLabel}</button>
          </form>
        </Show>
      </section>
    );
  },
});
```

The `required`, `type` and `maxlength` attributes let the browser catch most mistakes before anything is sent. The server checks again, since anyone can post anything.

## The handler

The handler is a plain function from a Fetch `Request` to a `Response`. It answers posts to its path and leaves every other request alone. After checking the fields it sends the message, then answers `303 See Other` with the page, a status and the brick's anchor, so the browser loads the page again with a GET and scrolls to the form.

```ts
// src/server/contact.ts (server only)
import { createLimiter, type Mailer } from '@mortar-kit/auth';

export function createContactHandler(options: { mailer: Mailer; to: string }) {
  const limiter = createLimiter(5, 10 * 60 * 1000); // 5 per client every 10 minutes
  return async (request: Request): Promise<Response | undefined> => {
    const url = new URL(request.url);
    if (url.pathname !== '/contact' || request.method !== 'POST') return undefined;
    const form = await request.formData();
    const page = samePath(form.get('return'), url); // a path on this site, else '/'
    const back = (status: string) =>
      new Response(null, { status: 303, headers: { location: `${page}?contact=${status}#contact` } });

    if (form.get('website')) return back('sent'); // the honeypot: a bot filled it in
    const ip = request.headers.get('x-forwarded-for')?.split(',')[0]?.trim();
    if (ip && limiter(ip) > 0) return back('busy');
    const message = check(form); // trimmed name, email and message, or undefined
    if (!message) return back('invalid');

    try {
      await options.mailer.send({
        to: options.to,
        replyTo: message.email,
        subject: `Contact form: ${message.name}`,
        text: message.message,
      });
    } catch {
      return back('failed');
    }
    return back('sent');
  };
}
```

Run it ahead of `createMiddleware`, in the site's `start.middleware`:

```ts
// src/middleware.ts
import { createMiddleware, type Middleware } from '@mortar-kit/auth';

const mortar = createMiddleware({ auth, blobs });
const handleContact = createContactHandler({ mailer, to: process.env.CONTACT_TO! });

const middleware: Middleware = async (event, next) => (await handleContact(event.request)) ?? mortar(event, next);
export default middleware;
```

Any `Mailer` works: `resendMailer` with the same settings as sign-in emails, `consoleMailer()` in development (it prints each message, reply address included), or a few lines over your own provider. `replyTo` puts the visitor's address on the email, so replying answers them.
